findings.body:
`assigned_variable_selector` is declared as `Sequence[str]` in `v1/node_data.py`. Pydantic keeps the value as a `list`, so `assigned_selector in variable_selectors` calls `hash()` on a list and raises `TypeError: unhashable type: 'list'`. I confirmed this in an isolated Python environment with pydantic 2.11.

The coordinator calls this hook for every executable node on each root-to-answer path (`coordinator.py` line 220). It has no exception handling there, and `_build_paths_map` runs inside `register`. So any v1 assigner on a path to an Answer that references a variable crashes the run. That is the exact scenario this PR targets, so the fix turns a streaming-order bug into a hard failure. The new regression test uses this path, so it should fail on this branch too.

::diff{file="api/core/workflow/nodes/variable_assigner/v1/node.py" lines="36-43"}

::callout{type="warning"}
Convert the selector to a tuple before the membership test, the same way the v2 node does with `tuple(item.variable_selector)`. Add a direct unit test for `blocks_variable_output` with a list-valued `assigned_variable_selector`. That test would have caught this without running the full graph.

::suggestion{file="api/core/workflow/nodes/variable_assigner/v1/node.py" lines="42-43"}
assigned_selector = tuple(self.node_data.assigned_variable_selector)
return assigned_selector in variable_selectors

The new test asserts that conversation-variable stream chunks are non-empty and equal the query. It never checks where those chunks sit relative to the assigner's completion event. The bug is about ordering: the Answer must not stream before the assignment is applied. A future regression that streams the new value after the assigner finishes but still emits chunks early would still pass, because it only checks values.

The test also omits `expected_event_sequence`, which the existing streaming test uses. Those two gaps together mean the test checks the output value but not the scheduling guarantee the PR is about. Compare the index of the assigner's `NodeRunSucceededEvent` with the index of the first conversation-variable chunk event.

::diff{file="api/tests/unit_tests/core/workflow/graph_engine/test_streaming_conversation_variables.py" lines="67-77"}

::callout{type="info"}
Since the test currently fails with the TypeError above, fix that first. Then strengthen the assertions so they pin the ordering.

The PR adds a `result_input_value` branch that reports `updated_variable.to_object()` for CLEAR mode and `income_value.to_object()` otherwise. The PR description says nothing about it, and no test covers it. For CLEAR, `updated_variable.value` is already set from the zero value, so the output is probably identical, but the change still alters the node's reported `inputs` for a feature the bug fix does not touch.

Unrelated behavior changes make the diff harder to review and hard to bisect later. Either drop this hunk from this PR or move it to a separate commit with its own test.

::diff{file="api/core/workflow/nodes/variable_assigner/v1/node.py" lines="102-111"}

::callout{type="info"}
If the change is intended, add a CLEAR-mode assertion on `inputs` and explain it in the commit message.

The new import is `from typing import Any, Set, Tuple`, and the signature uses `Set[Tuple[str, ...]]`. The v2 node in the same package uses the built-in `set[tuple[str, ...]]` and the rest of this file's own annotations already use built-in generics. The `typing.Set` and `typing.Tuple` aliases are deprecated since Python 3.9 and will likely be flagged by the ruff pyupgrade rules.

::diff{file="api/core/workflow/nodes/variable_assigner/v1/node.py" lines="1-2"}

::suggestion{file="api/core/workflow/nodes/variable_assigner/v1/node.py" lines="2-2"}
from typing import TYPE_CHECKING, Any

The diagnosis holds up. The v1 assigner writes the conversation variable and does not declare that to the coordinator, so the coordinator could stream an Answer that reads the old value. Adding the same `blocks_variable_output` contract that v2 already implements is the right shape. The fixture-based test is also the right kind of test, since it exercises the real coordinator and event flow instead of mocking it. Once the selector type issue is fixed, this is a clean, targeted fix.

::callout{type="info"}
The PR also leaves the existing `test_streaming_conversation_variables` test in place. Its fixture uses the v2 item shape, so it does not hit the new v1 override.
